 ### [Optimization and Parallelization](6233-optimization-and-parallelization.md)
-[Optimization and Parallelization](6233-optimization-and-parallelization.md) encompasses algorithmic strategies to accelerate the training of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) and extend its scalability to scenes exceeding single-GPU memory limits. It addresses the computational bottleneck of the differentiable rasterization backward pass and the memory constraints of large-scale scene reconstruction. It is distinguished from standard optimization by replacing the per-pixel gradient accumulation with per-splat accumulation to avoid atomic contention [5989-parallelization-for-backpropagation], partitioning Gaussian primitives across multiple GPUs for distributed training [7349-grendel-gs], and explicitly computing gradients for probabilistic masks during alpha blending [4254-maskgaussian-gradient-computation].
+This family encompasses methods that accelerate the training of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) and enable the optimization of extended representations, such as probabilistic masks or distributed parameter sets. It solves the computational bottlenecks of the standard differentiable rasterization pipeline, specifically the $O(N \cdot P)$ complexity of the backward pass and the memory limits of single-GPU training [6233-optimization-and-parallelization]. It is distinguished from standard optimization by restructuring the gradient flow (per-splat vs. per-pixel), extending the differentiable core to non-Gaussian parameters (masks), and distributing the state across hardware clusters [6233-optimization-and-parallelization].
 
-### [CLM](7351-clm.md)
-[CLM](7351-clm.md) is a training optimization method for 3D Gaussian Splatting that eliminates the performance bottleneck of frequent GPU memory barriers by dynamically migrating Gaussian states between CPU and GPU memory. It solves the problem of VRAM exhaustion and transfer latency during optimization by employing a smart caching policy that retains only the most frequently updated Gaussians on the GPU. Unlike [FastGS](9177-fastgs.md) which focuses on kernel fusion, or [Fused Backward and Optimizer](9597-fused-backward-and-optimizer.md) which optimizes the compute graph, CLM optimizes data placement.
